# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings
#
# This repo is developed on Windows, where `core.autocrlf=true` checks files
# out as CRLF. Prettier's `endOfLine: "lf"` then rewrites every one of them to
# LF, git converts them back on commit, and the working tree churns forever:
# `prettier --check` fails on ~700 files immediately after a fresh clone even
# though nothing is actually wrong.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the WORKING TREE to LF as well as the object store, which ends
# that loop regardless of each developer's core.autocrlf setting.
#
# After pulling this file once: `git add --renormalize .`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must stay LF even on Windows or the interpreter chokes.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts are the one exception that needs CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never convert, never diff as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.zip  binary
*.dump binary

# Generated / machine-owned: collapse in diffs and in GitHub's language stats.
pnpm-lock.yaml   linguist-generated=true -diff
skills-lock.json linguist-generated=true -diff
